<para/><item>Use the sim to model 6✕18.</item>

    <media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fbcd2c0d72399bc1a9f08f144315632f87b9fc23"/></media><para>What is the total area of this rectangle? Represent the total area in multiple ways. </para>



<para/><item>In an area model, how do the partial products (interior numbers) get calculated?</item>

<para/><item>In an area model, what are two different ways the total area could get calculated?</item>

<para/><item>How do the partial products relate to the total area of the area model?</item> 

</list>

<title>Part 3: Using an Area Model</title>
<para>Use the Explore Screen and Generic Screen to answer the following questions. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
<para/><item>Suppose we want to find the product of 17 and 13 using an area model.</item>

<list type="enumerated"><item>Draw and label your partitions on the scaled area model below.</item>

  <media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c0583c1e3de5ec8d5c2fb9f56878104dfede8ff0"/>

</media><item>Draw and label your partitions on the generic model below. </item> 

<media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5d7078d0d035592651c72ffe93732201515db380"/>

 

</media><para/><item>💬 Discuss with your group: How does your area model compare to those in your group? What is the same? What is different? </item>





<para/><item>Justify how you know your model represents 17x13. Does your area model represent 17✕13? How do you know? 💬</item>





</list>
<para/><item>Challenge yourself to work through levels 1-2 of the <link url="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/area-model-multiplication/latest/area-model-multiplication_en.html?screens=3">Area Model Numbers</link> Game!</item>

<para/><item>What are three different ways you could partition 17?</item> 

  <media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dd62f21e167876f8696008721ec10b849e46fe73"/>

</media><para/><item>Write your own 2-digit times 2-digit multiplication problem that uses an area model, and find the total area. </item>

<media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5d7078d0d035592651c72ffe93732201515db380"/>

</media><para/><item>What is a convenient way to break up a multiplication problem into an area model, and why is it convenient for you?</item>
<title>Part 4: Applying an Area Model </title>
<para>Use the Variables Screen to answer the following questions.</para> 

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated"><item>Play around with entering your own variable expressions, then answer the following. </item>

<list type="enumerated">
<para>
    </para><item>In an area model, how do the partial products (interior numbers) get calculated?</item>
    
    <item>In an area model, how does the total area get calculated?</item>
    
   <item> How do the partial products relate to the total area of the area model? </item>


</list>
  
<para>
  </para><item>Use the sim to model 6(x+8).<media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cf7bbb114082fb67e2ec1cc3f07fee7d54a3858a"/></media></item>
    
  <para>
  What is the total area of this rectangle? Represent the total area in multiple ways.💬

</para>

<para>
  </para><item>
    Use the sim to model (x+3)(x-5).<media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6b94112d13fd4e2278fecf9f89d55f70d1566205"/> </media></item>


<para/><item>Challenge yourself to work through levels 1 - 4 of the Area Model Algebra Game! </item>

<title>Summary</title>
<para/><item>How is multiplying <emphasis>variable expressions</emphasis> using an area model similar to multiplying <emphasis>numbers</emphasis> using an area model?</item> 
</list>



<title>Extension</title> 
<para>Challenge yourself to work backwards and factor an algebraic expression! 
<media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1d32b5338797703fac22f95c26199b0e97ec744a"/>
</media></para>
</list></section>
